@article{ WOS:001502343800029,
Author = {Juruna, Josiel Jacinto Pereira and Silva, Raimundo and dos Reis,
   Orcylene Barbosa and Moura Assuncao, Amildon and Sampaio da Silva,
   Anderson and Bezerra da Silva, Helio and Pereira Juruna, Jailson Jacinto
   and Abreu Nunes, Josimary and Souza Santos Kleme, Micaele and Passos
   Ferreira, Paulo and Txakui Viana da Silva Juruna, Ronald and Sousa dos
   Santos, Rosilene and Rodrigues Lima, Sara and Bezerra Lima, Sebastiao
   and Juruna da Cruz Pereira, Tarukawa and Quaresma, Adriano and Cunha de
   Queiroz, Alexya and Oliveira Sawakuchi, Andre and Ribas, Camila Cherem
   and Duarte Ritter, Camila and Costa Carneiro, Cristiane and Mileno Silva
   De Paula, Eder and Zuquim, Gabriela and Palmquist, Helena and Wahnfried,
   Ingo and Silva de Jesus, Jandessa and Muriel-Cunha, Janice and Zuanon,
   Jansen and Pezzuti, Juarez Carlos Brito and Silva de Medeiros, Marksuel
   Sandro and Lopes, Priscila F. M. and Mantovanelli, Thais Regina},
Title = {Socioenvironmental impacts of the Belo Monte hydroelectric power plant
   as revealed by Indigenous and ribeirinho monitoring},
Journal = {CONSERVATION BIOLOGY},
Year = {2025},
Volume = {39},
Number = {3},
Month = {JUN},
Abstract = {Hydroelectric dams, once seen as clean and renewable energy sources,
   have been the subject of extensive research, particularly concerning
   their socioenvironmental impacts. The Belo Monte hydroelectric power
   plant (HPP) relies on the operation of 2 dams that divert water from a
   130-km stretch of the Xingu River to generate energy. The dam has
   disrupted the seasonal flooding cycle (flood pulse) along the Volta
   Grande do Xingu (VGX) and created a reduced discharge condition
   analogous to a prolonged and extreme dry season in the watershed. Before
   the Belo Monte HPP, local communities relied on the highly diverse and
   abundant fish assemblage supported by seasonal flooding of the
   ecosystem. Local VGX residents sought partnerships and established the
   Independent Territorial Environmental Monitoring Program (MATI-VGX).
   Through this program, locals monitored fish spawning sites and fishing
   dynamics. This monitoring complemented and quantified local communities'
   perceptions about the environmental impacts caused by the Belo Monte
   HPP. The HPP was associated with a water discharge shortage that
   critically undermined the river's capacity to sustain vital ecosystem
   processes that support local people's lives. Drastic transformations of
   traditional lifestyles, shifts in fishing practices, and a significant
   decline in fishing yield occurred that jeopardized food sovereignty and
   security. The Belo Monte HPP environmental licensing process ignored
   local ecological knowledge and the vital links among the river's flood
   pulse, the aquatic and seasonally flooded ecosystems, and the
   traditional lifestyles of VGX residents. To ensure the ecological
   sustainability of the VGX, the Belo Monte HPP operation needs to change
   to support key spawning areas, maintain water discharge, avoid
   short-term water fluctuations, and emulate natural interannual discharge
   variability to mitigate flood pulse disruption. Local ecological
   knowledge should never be ignored in projects where local communities
   are the most affected. These communities should be central in
   decision-making regarding socioenvironmental impact assessment,
   mitigation, and monitoring.
   Impacto socio-ambiental de la central hidroel \& eacute;ctrica Belo
   Monte revelado por el monitoreo ind \& iacute;gena y ribeirinho Las
   presas hidroel \& eacute;ctricas, antes consideradas fuentes de energ \&
   iacute;a limpia y renovable, han sido objeto de numerosas
   investigaciones, sobre todo en lo que respecta a sus repercusiones
   socioambientales. La central hidroel \& eacute;ctrica de Belo Monte se
   basa en el funcionamiento de dos presas que desv \& iacute;an el agua de
   un tramo de 130 km del r \& iacute;o Xingu para generar energ \&
   iacute;a. La presa ha interrumpido el ciclo de inundaci \& oacute;n
   estacional (pulso de inundaci \& oacute;n) a lo largo del Volta Grande
   do Xingu (VGX) y ha creado una condici \& oacute;n de descarga reducida
   an \& aacute;loga a una estaci \& oacute;n seca prolongada y extrema en
   la cuenca. Antes de la central hidroel \& eacute;ctrica de Belo Monte,
   las comunidades locales depend \& iacute;an de la gran diversidad y
   abundancia de peces que proporcionaba la inundaci \& oacute;n estacional
   del bosque. Los residentes locales del VGX buscaron alianzas y
   establecieron el Programa de Vigilancia Ambiental Territorial
   Independiente (MATI-VGX). A trav \& eacute;s de este programa, la
   poblaci \& oacute;n local vigil \& oacute; los lugares de desove de los
   peces y la din \& aacute;mica de la pesca. Este seguimiento complement
   \& oacute; y cuantific \& oacute; las percepciones de las comunidades
   locales sobre los impactos ambientales causados por la central hidroel
   \& eacute;ctrica de Belo Monte. La central hidroel \& eacute;ctrica se
   asoci \& oacute; a una escasez de descarga de agua que redujo en gran
   medida la capacidad del r \& iacute;o para mantener procesos ambientales
   vitales que sustentan la vida de la poblaci \& oacute;n local. Esto caus
   \& oacute; transformaciones dr \& aacute;sticas en los estilos de vida
   tradicionales, cambios en las pr \& aacute;cticas pesqueras y una
   importante disminuci \& oacute;n del rendimiento de la pesca que
   pusieron en peligro la soberan \& iacute;a y la seguridad alimentarias.
   El proceso de autorizaci \& oacute;n ambiental de la central hidroel \&
   eacute;ctrica de Belo Monte ignor \& oacute; los conocimientos ecol \&
   oacute;gicos locales y los v \& iacute;nculos vitales entre el pulso de
   crecida del r \& iacute;o, los ecosistemas acu \& aacute;ticos y
   estacionalmente inundados y los estilos de vida tradicionales de los
   residentes del VGX. Para garantizar la sostenibilidad ecol \&
   oacute;gica del VGX, la operaci \& oacute;n de la central hidroel \&
   eacute;ctrica de Belo Monte debe cambiar para apoyar las zonas clave de
   desove, mantener la descarga de agua, evitar las fluctuaciones de agua a
   corto plazo y emular la variabilidad natural de la descarga interanual
   para mitigar la interrupci \& oacute;n del pulso de inundaci \&
   oacute;n. Los conocimientos ecol \& oacute;gicos locales nunca deben
   ignorarse en los proyectos en los que las comunidades locales son las m
   \& aacute;s afectadas. Estas comunidades deben ocupar un lugar central
   en la toma de decisiones relativas a la evaluaci \& oacute;n del impacto
   socio-ambiental, la mitigaci \& oacute;n y el seguimiento.
